fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "The Acceptable Year Of The Sun",
    "body": "Chance Garton opens on Psalm 19 and the phrase that gives the episode its name, locating the acceptable year of our Lord in Isaiah 61:2 and Luke 4:19, and noting that the synoptic gospels leave Jesus teaching for only a single year. Dylan Saccoccio then puts up a Plutarch passage dated to roughly 72 to 75 AD in which Helios is called Kyros, the Persian word for the sun, and argues that the phrase rendered spirit of the Lord in Luke 4:18 should read as spirit of the sun, since aion means a cycle of time and only the sun reckons a year."
  },
  {
    "title": "A Second Body Beside The Sun",
    "body": "Crrow777 walks the hosts through his own hydrogen alpha footage, which he dates to roughly 2015, showing a second body near the sun that he first assumed was a flare inside his etalon. He describes capping the scope with his hand and with a long cardboard tube to test it, credits Chris Van Matree with replicating the result in Colorado, and says Cammy Nodell's polarizing filter settled it. He points to a 2016 Slooh Observatory stream where an astronomer, with Mercury in transit, called the object our second sun, and he raises the Vedic claim of a solar binary as something still needing proof."
  },
  {
    "title": "Stars Supplied The Place Of History",
    "body": "Reading from page 299 of Robert Taylor's astronomical theological lectures, the three work through his chapter on the twelve patriarchs, where Taylor holds that the astronomical illustrations supplied the place of history and that each person was believed to have a star presiding over his nativity. Chance Garton adds Origen's commentary on Genesis, in which Jacob reads in the tables of heaven whatever happens to his children, and John McHugh's book The Celestial Code of Scripture, which describes a Mesopotamian practice called lumashi, the stars themselves read as a text, with Samson's jawbone traced to the donkeys near the constellation Cancer."
  },
  {
    "title": "Forgeries Guesswork And Lost Originals",
    "body": "Dylan Saccoccio contends that the Greek New Testament is a product of the Middle Ages, pointing to Christianos as a Latin formation in Greek letters, a point he credits to Archbishop Richard Trench, and to Erasmus filling in gaps in Revelation rather than leaving them blank. Crrow777 raises The Worship of Augustus Caesar and its account of monuments recut with hammer and chisel, plus the Lamsa Bible on idiom. Chance Garton notes Pope Leo's bounty in gold for ancient texts and the forgeries that followed, while Saccoccio corrects the astrology line pinned on J. P. Morgan as absent from the record."
  },
  {
    "title": "Miriam White As Snow",
    "body": "The closing stretch turns on Taylor's imagined priest pointing east at stars rising from a sea reddened by the sun, naming the bright one Moses, a dimmer one Aaron, and the pale one Miriam, the Venus Anadyomene of the pagan and the Virgin Mary of the Christian fable. Dylan Saccoccio reads the leprosy of Numbers 12:10 as figurative language for whiteness rather than disease, tying it to Venus rising in Taurus while the sun blots out the constellation it enters, and he stresses that he writes the allegory is claimed to be, because the claim belongs to the priesthood and not to him."
  }
]

